"""Chapter 20 presenter notes, version 2.0.0: the notes carry the lecture (ruling 2 of 2026-11-14): 60 to 140 words on a content slide, at least 30 on title, part and
resources slides, in full sentences with no label prefixes."""
__version__ = "2.0.0"

NOTES = {
    "title": "Welcome to chapter 20, on sending email, texts and push notifications from a program. There are nineteen examples and they all run on the chapter page, in the order of this deck. None of them needs an account or a network: Google's service is replaced by a stand-in object, the mail server and the notification server run on this machine, and every slide that does this says so. Each part begins with an everyday picture and some parts with a true story.",
    "route": "The chapter has five parts. First the Gmail API, which is the easy way to send and read mail today. Then mail as a protocol, with smtplib, so that you see what really travels. Then texts sent through email gateways. Then push notifications with ntfy, which replaced the gateways for many people. Last, three practice programs. The table gives the slide numbers and how many examples each part holds, and the chapter-code tab of the page lists the same examples in the same order.",
    "questions": "Two questions will guide the whole chapter. The first asks how a program sends a message to a person without lying about who it is from, and without sending it to the wrong people. The second asks how it does so without putting a secret in the wrong place, such as a password in a file, a topic name in a public list, or a command a stranger can choose. Keep both in mind. The summary and the practice questions come back to them at the end.",
    "divider1": "Part one is about the Gmail API through the ezgmail module. You will see why a program needs two files before it can send anything, the credentials Google gives you once and the token it saves for you, and what the first sign-in does. Then you send, read, search, reply and download attachments. Every example here uses a stand-in for Google's service, so you can see exactly what the module hands over without touching a real mailbox.",
    "divider2": "Part two goes under the hood. A mail is a set of headers and a body, built with the email package, and sent with smtplib by talking to a server in short commands. You will see how accents are written, why a line break in a header is refused, how STARTTLS encrypts the conversation, how a server refuses a recipient, and how the envelope differs from the headers. The last fact explains how a blind copy works.",
    "divider3": "Part three covers texts sent by email. A carrier can turn mail sent to a special address into a text on a phone, and the book lists the addresses in a table. You will build such an address and count how many text messages a given text really needs. Then a story shows that this door can be closed by its owner, and a table shows what became of the addresses of the book.",
    "divider4": "Part four is about push notifications with ntfy. The idea is simple: you send one web request to a topic, and every phone that subscribes to that topic shows the message. There is no account in the simple case, so the name of the topic is the only secret. You will publish a message, add a title and a priority in headers, read the stream that comes back, and then see why a short topic name is a weak password.",
    "divider5": "Part five has three small programs that put the earlier parts together. An unsubscriber that finds the safe link in a mail, an umbrella reminder that reads a forecast and decides by the chance of rain, and a program that obeys commands in mail, with a password and a list of allowed commands. After them come the practice questions, the summary and the resources. Each program uses calls you have already seen.",
    "A1": "A post-office box needs a key that you are given once. That key is credentials.json, which Google issues when you enable the API. The first time you sign in, you get a visitor pass that expires, and that is token.json, which the module saves so that you do not sign in again. The clerk behind the counter hands over only what the pass allows, and that limit is the scope. Every other concept of the part is a task at that counter: send, read, search, download.",
    "A2": "The envelope is what the postman reads, and the header of the letter is what the reader sees. The two can differ, and that is how a blind copy works. A registered-mail counter will refuse a letter addressed wrongly, and that is a server refusing a recipient. The slide also lists the small tools around the protocol: logging to keep a record of the conversation, datetime for the date header, and imaplib, the module that reads a mailbox directly.",
    "A3": "A carrier's mail-to-text address is a side door into the phone network. You write a letter to the number at the carrier's own domain, and the carrier turns it into a text on the phone. The door belongs to the carrier, so the carrier can lock it without asking you. The table of the book lists the doors, and the next slides count how many texts a message needs and what became of the doors.",
    "A4": "A noticeboard in a corridor works like ntfy. Anyone who knows the name of the board can pin a note on it, and anyone who knows the name can read it, so the name is the secret. The title, the priority and the tags are the pin, the colour and the sticker on the note. The requests module is the hand that pins the note, and json is how you read the list of notes that the board returns.",
    "A5": "A kitchen timer, a stopwatch and a doorman give the pictures for the practice programs. The umbrella reminder is a window you look out of before you leave. The unsubscriber is a person who walks through the junk mail and finds the one safe way to stop it. The program that obeys mail is a doorman who needs a password and checks a short list of allowed requests. Beautiful Soup, subprocess and the torrent client appear in the book's versions of these programs.",
    "setup": "Before a program can use Gmail it needs two files. The first is credentials.json, which you download from Google once, after enabling the API for your project. The second is token.json, which the module creates after the first sign-in in a browser. Here the folder is empty, so the call to init stops with an exception that names the missing file. That is the most common first error, and the message is clear if you read it. The printed scope shows that the program asks for full access to the mailbox, and that nobody is signed in yet.",
    "send": "ezgmail.send takes the recipient, the subject and the body, and nothing else is needed. To show what happens without a mailbox, a stand-in object replaces Google's service, and it remembers its last call. We then decode what ezgmail handed to it. Gmail wants the whole message as one base64 text, so decoding it gives back an ordinary email whose To, From and Subject are exactly what we typed. The From line is the account the module signed in with. Because this is a stand-in, no message was sent to anyone.",
    "read": "Searching returns threads, and each thread holds messages. A message has a sender, a subject and a body that you can read as plain attributes. The search text is passed to Gmail unchanged, so everything you can type in the Gmail search box works here, for example from:, has:attachment or a word. The stand-in service returns one thread that holds one message, built the way Google's API builds it, with headers as pairs of names and values and the body as base64. The last line shows that the query reached the service as typed.",
    "reply": "Replying is a send with two things done for you. The answer goes to the sender of the mail, and it is filed in the same thread, which is why the thread number printed is the one of the original message. The subject is kept as it was. Then the body is the text we gave. The stand-in service lets us read what would have been sent. Notice what is not promised: the module does not write a Re: prefix for you, so check how your program's replies look in a real mailbox before you rely on them.",
    "download": "A mail can carry an attachment, and the attachment has a name chosen by the sender. In this message the name starts with two dots and a slash, which means the folder above. We ask the module to save the attachment into the inbox folder, and it writes the file one level up, outside the folder we chose. This is a path traversal, and it is a real finding of this study. The lesson is general: a name you receive from outside is data, not a safe file name, so clean it before you write a file.",
    "build": "The email package builds a message as a tree. The top is a multipart mixed container, inside it a multipart alternative holds the plain text and the HTML version of the same body, and the attachment sits beside it. walk visits every part in that order, so the list shows five content types. iter_attachments finds the attachment and its file name. get_body asks which part is best to show, and with HTML preferred it answers with the HTML part. Building mail with this class is safer than gluing strings together, because it writes the headers correctly.",
    "headers": "Headers are plain ASCII on the wire, so the email package encodes an accented subject as a short encoded word, and the first line shows it. The address with a name is written by formataddr and read back by parseaddr, which splits the name from the address. The last step is a defence: if a value could carry a line break, a second header, for example a hidden copy to a stranger, could be added. The package refuses such a value with an error. Never build headers by gluing together text that came from outside.",
    "starttls": "A connection to a mail server begins in plain text, and STARTTLS is the request to switch to an encrypted one. The client says hello, the server lists what it can do and offers STARTTLS, the client asks for it, and the code 220 says go ahead. From then on everything is encrypted, and the server's own report confirms it saw TLS. The client also checks the certificate, which here comes from a one-day test certificate made with openssl. Never turn that check off to make an error go away, because it is the part that protects you.",
    "refusals": "A mail server can refuse a recipient with a code, and here a stand-in server refuses one domain with 550. If only some recipients are refused, sendmail still delivers to the others and returns a dictionary of the refused ones, so check it. If all are refused, smtplib raises SMTPRecipientsRefused, which carries the same dictionary. And if no server is listening, you get a connection error before any mail exists. Three outcomes, three different ways your program must react, and none of them is a silent success.",
    "envelope": "Two lists of recipients exist in every mail. The headers say who the mail is for, and the envelope says who the server delivers it to. Here the header names only Jon, but the call adds a second address, and the server delivers to both. The text the receiver sees contains no sign of the second person, and that is exactly how a blind copy works. It also explains why the From header of a mail is not proof of who sent it: the envelope and the headers are written by the sender and checked by nobody.",
    "gateway": "A gateway address is the phone number at the carrier's mail domain. Building one is a plain string join. The two lines of the run show two limits. First, two carriers can share one domain, so you cannot find the carrier from the address. Second, the addresses can be switched off: AT&T ended its email-to-text on 17 June 2025, so the first row of the book's table has been dead for the number of days the last line prints. The chapter itself warns that what works today may not work tomorrow.",
    "segments": "A text message is not as long as you think. In the seven-bit alphabet of the phone network, one message holds 160 characters, and 140 bytes of eight bits are exactly 160 characters of seven bits. A longer message is cut into parts, and each part holds 153 characters, because some space goes to the header that puts the parts in order. Use a character outside the alphabet, like the Turkish dotless i, and the whole message moves to a sixteen-bit coding that holds only 70 characters, or 67 per part. Count in the coding the phone uses, not the characters Python shows.",
    "ntfyPub": "Publishing a notification with ntfy is one web request. The topic is the path of the address, and the message is the body. There is no account and no key in this simple case. We use a small recorder on this machine instead of ntfy.sh, and it keeps what it receives. The run shows the status 200, the path with the topic name, the body, and the length of the body. Nothing else identifies the receiver, so anyone who knows the topic name can read and write it, which is the point of the next slides.",
    "ntfyHdr": "The title, the priority and the tags of a notification are sent as headers, and the message stays in the body. The recorder received all three exactly as we set them. The last lines show a limit that is easy to forget. Headers must be written in Latin-1, so a dotless i raises UnicodeEncodeError before anything is sent. If a title needs such characters, put them in the body instead, or encode them as the service documents. A header is the wrong place for free text.",
    "ntfyPoll": "Receiving works the other way: you ask the topic for what it holds, and you get one JSON object per line. We typed such an answer into the program so the run never changes. You parse each line separately with json.loads. The first event is not a message but an open, which tells you the stream began, so you keep only the events whose kind is message. The time is epoch seconds, as in chapter 19, so datetime turns it into a clock time. And an empty answer has no line to parse, so check before you index.",
    "secrecy": "With ntfy, the topic name is the password, so its strength matters. A name of 18 mixed letters and digits is about 107 bits at most, and a human-chosen name is far weaker in practice. An 8-letter name has about 48 bits, and that can be guessed by a program. The secrets module makes a name with 144 bits from the system's random source. One more limit: the message size is counted in bytes, so twenty characters with one accented letter take twenty-one bytes. Count bytes, not characters, when you work near a limit.",
    "unsub": "The practice program finds the link that unsubscribes you. The book uses Beautiful Soup, and here the parser of the standard library does the same: it remembers the address of each link and keeps it when the link's text says unsubscribe. Well-made mail also carries a List-Unsubscribe header with a mail address and a web address, which is the safe way to find it. The program does not open anything here. Take care in real life: only unsubscribe from senders you know, because for a spammer a click proves that your address is alive.",
    "umbrella": "The umbrella reminder reads a forecast and decides. The forecast is typed in as a list of periods, in the shape a weather service returns, so that the run never changes. The lesson is how to decide. The word rain does not appear in the text Showers And Thunderstorms, so searching for the word fails, while the chance of precipitation says sixty. And a period with no chance has the value None, which must be treated as zero before it is compared. Decide by the number, with a rule you can state: fifty or more means take an umbrella.",
    "control": "The book's last program starts a download when it receives a mail. This is the most dangerous idea of the chapter, because whoever can send mail to the program can make it run things. The safe form has two checks. The password is compared in constant time, and the command must be on a short list of allowed commands, each stored as a list of arguments. A wrong password stops first, a command that is not on the list stops second, and only an allowed command runs. Never pass text from a mail to a shell.",
    "compare_gateways": "This table gathers what the study found about the gateway domains of the book's table, by a DNS query for their mail servers. The two domains of AT&T and Cricket's text address had none, two of Sprint's answered that they do not exist, one of Boost failed, and twelve had mail servers. A missing mail server is a symptom, not proof, so the takeaway says so. The carriers' own notices are the evidence: AT&T ended its service on 17 June 2025, and Verizon expects its shutdown of vtext.com and vzwpix.com to be complete by 31 March 2027.",
    "practice": "Try to answer each question before you run anything, as you would guess the weather before you look out of the window. Then check on the chapter page. The five questions cover the whole chapter: what the Gmail module needs before it can send, what the envelope is and how it differs from the headers, how many texts a message needs, how a topic name acts as a password, and how a program that obeys mail stays safe. Each question names the slide where the idea was shown.",
    "summary": "Five sentences carry the chapter. The Gmail API needs two files, and a message you receive is data, not a safe file name. A mail is headers and a body, and the envelope decides who gets it. A text is counted in the coding the phone uses, and its gateway can be switched off. A notification topic is a password, so give it enough randomness. And a program that obeys mail needs a password and a list of allowed commands. The chart counts the examples of each part.",
    "resources": "These fifteen links are where to go next. They include the chapter itself, the documentation of the modules, the protocol and the service pages, and the pages behind the stories. The Gmail guide, the ntfy documentation and the RFC are primary sources, and the Wikipedia pages are good for the history. Every link was opened and answered on 12 November 2026. There is no video and no community link yet, which the lineage records as a gap, so say if you want them added.",
}

STORY_NOTES = {
    "imap": "In 1986 Mark Crispin at Stanford designed IMAP, a protocol that keeps the mail on the server. The widely used POP simply fetched a mailbox. With IMAP several programs can share one mailbox, because nothing leaves the server until a client deletes it. The first client ran on a Xerox Lisp machine against a TOPS-20 server, and the improved IMAP2 became RFC 1064 in 1988 and RFC 1176 in 1990. The picture is a Lisp machine of the kind in the story. The lesson: IMAP leaves mail on the server, so many programs can share one mailbox.",
    "tomlinson": "Ray Tomlinson at Bolt, Beranek and Newman wrote the first email program that could send mail between users on different hosts of the ARPANET, in 1971. Before that, mail could reach only people on the same computer. He needed a sign to separate the user name from the machine name, and he chose the @ because nobody used it in user names or in TENEX programs. The picture is a map of the ARPANET. The lesson: every address your program builds still has Tomlinson's @ in it.",
    "rfc821": "In August 1982 Jonathan Postel of the Information Sciences Institute published RFC 821, which defined the Simple Mail Transfer Protocol. The page of the RFC now says it is obsolete, replaced by RFC 2821 and then by RFC 5321 of October 2008. Yet smtplib still speaks the same conversation: a greeting, then MAIL FROM and RCPT TO. The picture is a diagram of the SMTP transfer model. The lesson: mail is an old protocol, so trust nothing in it that is not checked.",
    "spam": "In 1978 Gary Thuerk sent the first known spam, an advertisement for a presentation of the Digital Equipment Corporation, over the ARPANET, where commercial mail was forbidden. Counts of recipients differ between sources, so the slide says several hundred. He was reprimanded. Only slightly more than half of the intended people received it, because of software limits. The picture shows a machine of the family DEC made. The lesson: volume is judged by the receiver, so send little and say who you are.",
    "sms": "On 3 December 1992 Neil Papworth, an engineer of Sema Group, sent the first text, Merry Christmas, from a computer to Richard Jarvis's phone on the Vodafone network in the United Kingdom. The length was limited so that a message fits the signalling formats that already existed, first 128 bytes and then 160 characters of seven bits. The picture is a phone showing a text message. The lesson: count the characters in the coding the phone uses, not in Python.",
    "att": "AT&T told its customers that on 17 June 2025 it shut down its email-to-text and text-to-email service and gave no reason. Commentators suggest that it was old, open to abuse and free, but these are their opinions. A DNS query in this study found no mail servers for its two gateway domains. The picture is drawn for this deck from that measurement, because no free picture of it exists. The lesson: a gateway the carrier owns can be switched off without asking you.",
}
